{
 "id": "2025-sorokowski-ornament",
 "name": "Ornament Preference for Plates and Shirts",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Sorokowski, P., Luty, J., Jędryczka, W., & Stefańczyk, M. M. (2025). Is ornamentation a universal human preference? Cross-cultural and developmental evidence from Scotland, Pakistan, and Papua. Evolutionary Psychology, 23(4). https://doi.org/10.1177/14747049251388596\n\nWhat participants do: participants see six pairs of everyday objects, first three pairs of plates and then three pairs of T-shirts (pair order random within each block). In each pair one object is plain and the other carries an ornament (magnolia flowers, a leafy twig or an abstract pattern), and the ornament is on the left in three pairs and on the right in three, exactly as in the paper's Figure 1. For each pair they choose the one they like better, or say they like both equally (coded 1 left, 2 equal, 3 right). The page recodes each answer as 1 = plain preferred, 2 = no preference, 3 = ornamented preferred and sums them into the paper's scores: plates 3–9, shirts 3–9, total 6–18. Then age, sex, country and type of place of residence.\n\nOriginal finding (215 parent–child dyads from Scotland, Pakistan and Papua New Guinea): ornamented objects were generally preferred; the preference was stronger in the less WEIRD societies, and only in the Western sample did it decline with age. Adult men preferred ornamentation more than adult women.\n\nStimuli: the 12 pictures from Figure 1 of the article, used with credit to the authors. This template is removed if the authors object.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) Instructions and the consent text are paraphrased; the paper gives only the task description. (2) Parent and child are not linked as a dyad: one person answers per response, and a \"who is answering\" question (adult / child with a parent nearby) is added. (3) Place of residence is asked as country plus city / town / village. (4) The pictures are cut from the published figure, so they are smaller than the originals.",
